        "text": "In blockchains, the Byzantine General's problem is mitigated by allowing all users on the network to validate the information. In the interest of precision, we'll stop referring to users as users and call them nodes instead. In the decentralized environment we're trying to create, nodes don't, or at least shouldn't, trust their peers. You never know which one could turn malicious, or whether they're unknowingly passing you false information. To combat the spread of false information, we use a blockchain to sequence the messages as they appear. If nodes want to submit a message, which in our case is a block, then they need to put in some work to prove that they're serious. This is the logic behind proof of work, a type of consensus mechanism allowing nodes to coordinate in a distributed setting. Such mechanisms ensure that all agents in the system can agree on a single truth, even if some agents fail.",
        "start": 240,
        "end": 52640,
        "confidence": 0.98289996,
        "channel": null,
